The board configuration and piece counts confirm that it is player o's turn, with all columns except column 2 legal. Exhaustive examination of the six legal drop squares shows that o has no immediate 4-in-a-row, and likewise that x has no immediate winning reply. Consequently the position must be evaluated by the threats each side can create on subsequent moves.

The empty cell (0,4) already completes a diagonal of three o-pieces, so it is a terminal threat for o once it becomes reachable. The empty cell (2,4) likewise completes a diagonal of three x-pieces, so it is a terminal threat for x once it becomes reachable. Column 4 is therefore the unique “hot” column whose first two empty cells control both of those terminal threats.

Placing an o-piece at (1,1) simultaneously:

- creates an open three on row 1 whose right-hand extension is precisely (1,4);
- opens the subsequent drop (0,1).

From (0,1) the diagonal (0,1)–(1,2)–(2,3)–(3,4) becomes an open three whose free cell is the currently legal drop (3,4). Thus the single move in column 1 manufactures a forced sequence:

- o answers any non-blocking reply by dropping at (0,1), threatening (3,4);
- x is compelled to take (3,4), thereby opening his own terminal threat at (2,4);
- o blocks at (2,4), which now makes (1,4) a terminal threat (the row-1 four);
- x is compelled to take (1,4);
- o finishes at (0,4) and wins.

The only way x can avoid the above line is to answer the first move by occupying (0,1) himself. Even after that defence, o can switch to the symmetric left-hand extension of the same row-1 three (cells (3,0) then (2,0)). That extension again produces a terminal threat that x must answer, after which the original column-4 complex remains available as a residual odd-row threat. In every variation, therefore, column 1 seizes the initiative and leaves x unable to create a matching double threat of his own.

No other opening column generates an analogous pair of coupled terminal threats, so column 1 is the unique optimal move.

Final Answer: 1

